 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "How it works",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The voltage is set by a buried Zener, free of the surface noise found in ordinary Zeners. A high-gain shunt regulator on the chip surrounds the Zener and lowers its dynamic impedance; the whole is temperature compensated. Electrically it behaves as a two-terminal Zener: cathode positive."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Operating current",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The reference is specified from 0.6 mA to 15 mA of reverse current; the absolute limits are 30 mA reverse and 2 mA forward. Fed from a supply through a series resistor RS, the resistor is sized for at least 0.6 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Internal circuit",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Inside are a 6.2 V Zener (D1), seven transistors (Q1 to Q7), resistors of 50 Ω, 1 kΩ, 2 kΩ (two), 2.6 kΩ, 10 kΩ and 30 kΩ, and capacitors of 15 pF and 30 pF, all between terminal 1 (+) and terminal 2 (-)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Grades and packages",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Grades differ only in temperature coefficient: A 10 ppm/°C, B 20, C 50 and LM329D 100 ppm/°C, each a maximum at 1 mA over the operating range. The LM329 is rated 0 °C to 70 °C. The LM129 (-55 °C to 125 °C) and the TO-46 can are obsolete; the TO-92 is the package offered in its place."
  }
 ],
